A||||4||enslaved||Brake light prob||||1||
Z||000000||enslaved||08-21-2001||10:52 PM||||When i was in the process of buying my car.. i had it inspected before i bought it. When i took it, i found out the typical stuff. But one thing i noticed after i bought it, is the BRAKE light is staying on, on the console. Now, the e-brake line is supposedly frozen.. at least thats what they told me.. but the brake handle is disengaged all the way down. When i lift the handle up a little ways and set it in just the right spot... it turns off. but when i let go of the button and return it back down all the way... it turns back on... WTF? how can i fix this? And one other thing.. When i pull the e-brake up a little ways.. i still feel tension on it like a normal car.. that doesn't mean that the break is necesarily working.. right? thanks in advance!||||reg||1
Z||000001||enslaved||08-21-2001||11:39 PM||||el bumpo? hehe||||reg||1
Z||000002||hyhy4u||08-21-2001||11:44 PM||||Theres pertty much just a short in the wire that runs to the brake..if ya want the light off like i did..just pull the E. Brake boot off, which requires taking that trim piece off.  Once you got the boot off there will be a plug at the end of the mechinism..unplug it and leave it just hang there..that will turn the light off..i actually did mine cause i had a problem with the emissions testing..they wouldnt test my car with the Brake light on..what a bunch of B.S!, lol. That should just turn it off..not really fix it but in a way yes, lol..If that dont work then you gotta mess with the plug thats under your hood that plugs in after the master cylinder.<P>-Jason-||||reg||1
Z||000003||enslaved||08-22-2001||12:07 AM||||thanks for your help man, i'll have to give that a shot! lol. I wish there was a easy way to fix my e-brake.. im just affraid its engaged a little and screwing my calipers and rotors up!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-22-2001||01:39 PM||||If you are sure the e-brake does not work then just loosen the cable adjuster (located under the engine cradle crossmember) so you are sure the calipers are not dragging.<P>Looking at the back side of the rear caliper you will see the e-brake apply lever with the cable end attached.  If the lever is resting against the stop then it should not be dragging.  If it is not completely against the stop then loosen the cable adjuster until it is against the stop.<P>||||reg||1
